1. e4 e6 { This study contains all the French lines (with Nc3 and Nce2 c3 being the absolute main line/our preferred choice) and some transpositions leading to a French or a French like position (we try to get as much as possible some Nc3 french, advance or pseudo french Scandinavians, in this order).
Fundamentally, according to the CCD, the French is a draw with perfect.
For me this is a bit hard to accept as I always thought deep down that I can win after e4 e6 if I play well (because classically e6 instead of e5 is already a slight concession from black tempo wise and from a positional point of view. Tempo wise meaning if black ever wants to replace his e pawn on the better square e5 in the future, he would have wasted a tempo), but apparently this is not guaranteed (with perfect play). Practically as white, we can still aim towards a very comfortable and ever lasting positional advantage out of the French, typically with the Nc3 Nce2 c3 line. } 2. d4 { Ideally, we should look at 5 to 10 black possible moves here. d5 is a no-brainer/most played move (at 99% of OTB master+ games) but also more strange moves like a6, c5 b6 d6 g6 or Nf6 exist.
Fundamentally, the French is bad for black not because it is objectively a bad opening (the CCD already claims a forced draw after 1...e6 no matter what white plays, including 2.d4), but because white has a lot of choices / flexibility (asides from the very well known and studied main lines : Nc3, the advance french or the Winawer to name a few extremely played setups). The French is bad because white has a lot of flexibility/choice on what type of strong system he wants to for at multiple points in the opening. To get a taste of this extreme white flexibly in the French, just after 2.d4 d5 white has already 6 drawing moves at move 2, the worse of 6, Bb5+ c6 Bd3 still draws for white.
The 6 drawing attempts ranked (somewhat arbitrarily) for the CCD are : exd5 Nc3 Nd2 Bd3 e5 and Bb5+. } 2... d5 *


[Event "White Vs [French]: Opening [e4 e6 d4 c6 ] A tempo up French"]
