[ChapterName "Opening [e4 e6 d4 c6 ] A tempo up French"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Gg4E2sIS/TpZEYvmo"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/MansourMohamed"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/4p3/8/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.04.09"]
[UTCTime "13:19:36"]

1... c6? { The first thing to note is that we can obtain this position from a French move order (1.e4 e6 2.d4 c6) or a Caro-Kann move order (1.e4 c6 2.d4 e6). This elementary fact by itself makes studying this position a bit more appealing. By that logic, this could be viewed as an opening (vs the French) or as a transposition (out of the Caro-Kann).
Before looking at any engine lines, conceptually the e6 and c6 combo is bad on its own. When you think of any scenario where the c6 c5 e6 pawn structure is good for black, our only reference point is some semi-slave, QGD or Catalan type of positions. But this already does not work because white has already achieved e4, a dream move in all these opening where white goes e3 first and prepares e4 for a lot of moves and trying to make it work is a long strategic battle. If black ends up in a French/type structure, he will be loosing a whole tempo for free because often c7 c5 is a critical break/resource for black, while here it will be done in two moves instead of one. If black wants a Caro-Kann, including e6 and walling his LSB means he will get the worst of both worlds between the French and the Caro-Kann.
Might seem anecdotal to study, since d5 is played at 99% for OTB master+ games.
However, this particular line will lead to some main line french positions with a free tempo up for white (which stems from c7 c6 and c6 c5 in two tempi instead of one).
Here are some important sample lines :
Nf3 d5 Nbd2 Nf6 e5 Nfd7 Bd3 c5 c3 which a tempo up main line Tarrasch French.
Nf3 d5 e5 c5 c3 Nc6 Bd3 which is a tempo up main line advance French.
Bd3 d5 e5 c5 c3 which a tempo up main line advance French.
Bd3 Nf6 Nc3 d5 e5 Ne7 Nce2 c5 c3 which is a tempo up Nc3 Nce2 c3 french main line.
In all these versions, white gets a move for free, which he uses for Bd3 in good conditions in all 4.
Aside from some practical applications, these lines are great to build our intuition about the French plans and piece placement if we are half a move ahead.
For white without any prior knowledge,c4 might be temping to prevent d5, but it does not really prevent d5 as black can still go d5 anyway. The line goes c5 d5 and after cxd5, if black goes cxd5 we end up in a main line advance French position, but black can also deviate with exd5, and we are in some Caro-Kann side line that it barely good for white.
Since we do not play the Tarrasch as our white repertoire (basically a Nd2 French), the most logical moves here are Bd3 or Nf3 and maybe Nc3 to get a better version of the advance french, which we do prepare with great depth or a better version of the Nc3 Nce2 c3 french which is our main white repertoire vs the French.
One key difference between Nf3 and Bd3 is that Nf3 denies the pawn gambit line with Qxe4 at some point, while Bd3 first allow it. Bd3 first on the other hand keeps the possibility of getting a maximal Nc3 Nce2 c3 French version, which is really appealing for white.
Engines recommend Nf3 or Bd3, with Nf3 being slightly better than Bd3, but both win with perfect play.
While browsing the CCD, Nc3 is also a main move here, and the goal for white is to get the Qxd4 pawn gambit line.
For now I choose Nf3 as my main repertoire here. } { [%csl Re4,Re5,Rd5,Rd4][%cal Gc2c4,Rd7d5,Bg1f3,Bf1d3] } 2. Nf3! { Nf3 is objectively the slightly better move than Bd3 according to engines.
The more I think about it, Nf3 is a very good because white is the one to choose which line he wants after d5, so he can go for the advance French line if he wants and avoid any Tarrash business all together. } (2. Bd3 { Bd3 first is super attractive to me as Nc3 Nce2 c3 french player. In that line, we miss a tempo to play Bd3 first and are forced to play our knight maneuver to e2, blocking our LSB on f1.
With Bd3 first I might get both positional advantages : Bd3 which the thematic french square for our LSB especially with the c3 d4 e5 chain, and we might even get the the time for Nc3 Nce2 as a deep and powerful rerouting maneuver for our b1 knight typical in the Nc3 French line.
A sample line that achieves both these goals is : Nf6 Nc3 d5 e5 Nd7 Nce2 c5 c3. } 2... d5 { Here we have the choice between three moves e5, Nc3 or Nd2.
First let's look at Nd2. The engine PV looks insane: Nd2 c5 Ngf3 c4 Be2. Allowing c5 without playing c3 to go cxd4 on cxd4 is already a bad sign, as making c5 work is often times the sign that black equalizes any french position. But also allowing c4 from black means that the position now becomes very special and weird, and it needs very precise play and understanding from white to prove the advantage.
e5 right away is the most straightforward approach as it will lead us directly into a tempo up main line advance french position, which should be the dream for white. A sample line is e5 c5 c3, and we are already there.
Nc3 is the riskier but higher ceiling move and is the move we want to play as a Nc3 Nce2 c3 french player. Conceptually, the ideal sample line is Nc3 Nf6 e5 Ne7 Nce2 c5 c3. And white has achieved everything he wanted out of the opening. But there is a slight annoyance after Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 Qxd4 Nf3. White has lost a pawn and is ahead and development and thus should be better (at around +0.7 eval). But the position is a bit dissimilar from a normal french position and thus requires some specific preparation to really know how to navigate it. } (2... Nf6 { Here, Nc3 is the principled approach to get a better version of the Nc3 french main line (we included Bd3 for free). Engine also like e5 Nd5 c4 and claims it wins even more than Nc3. } 3. Nc3 (3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 Nb4 5. Be2) 3... d5 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Nce2) 3. e5 (3. Nc3 Nf6 (3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Qxd4 5. Nf3 { The so called Qxd4 gambit line. Note that we could obtain this with many move orders, Nc3 instead of Bd3, for example. Also note that this gambit is sound for white, as white will get enough activity and fast development as compensation for the pawn. }) 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Nce2 c5 6. c3 { This was our goal position after c6 white is doing objectively really well because he has 3 positional trumps: the French structure, the Nc3 Nce2 maneuver which is great as it redirects the b1 knight into better square and is int time ton consolidate the pawn chain with c3 on c5 (which is often a luxury in many French position). The third luxury is to get to play Bd3 which is inherently good in a French position in conjunction with the c3 d4 e5 pawn chain but also because we never actually get both Nc3 Nce2 c3 and Bd3 in the main line Nc3 french because Nc3 Nce2 will normally block the diag of our LSB on f1 for a long time. } 6... Nc6 { Here, a lot of moves are viable for white. If we compare this to the main line Nc3 Nce2 c3 French, the typical moves we would expect here are either f4 or Nf3. These moves are still good here and on the CCD the engines also recommend h4 a3 and Be3.
Personally, I lean towards f4 to get a similar yet arguably strictly better version of the main line Nc3 Nce2 c3 french that is my main white repertoire vs the French. } 7. f4) 3... c5 4. c3) (2. Nc3 d5 3. Bd3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Qxd4 5. Nf3 { We could have also gotten this gambit of a pawn with Bd3 first instead of Nc3. }) 2... d5 { Here Nbd2 is possible to get a tempo up Tarrash, but we are very happy with e5 to get a tempo up advance french an opening we know a lot more.
Bd3 is tempting but Bd3 dxe4 Bxd3 Nf6 Bd3 is not very good for white (+0.2 for white at lower depth while } 3. e5 { e5 is critical to avoid going to any Tarrash lines which I do not prepare. } (3. Nbd2 { For now, I do not recommend it because of the simple reason that I do not play the Tarrash so a fortiori I won't know how to play a tempo up Tarrash. Instead, I prefer lines with Bd3 (in general) or with e5 the previous move to get an advance french with a free tempo, an opening I studied a lot. Studying the Tarrash for the sole purpose of playing this very rare position is also a tough sell simply because this is not an EXACT main line Tarrash. } 3... Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3) 3... c5 4. c3 Nc6 { Notice that this is a main line advance French position with half a tempo up for white (here it's white's turn to move while it should be black's turn). } 5. Bd3 Qb6 { Here, like in the advance french main line, we know that d4 is tactically protected so the most logical continuation for white here is O-O. } 6. O-O Bd7 { A quick little nuance here:
First idea is to that we can go Be2 back, as from our French knowledge we know that Bd7 counters Bd3 and that Be2 is good vs Bd7. If we go Be2, white has wasted a tempo with Bd3 Be2 and black wasted a tempo earlier with c7 c6 into c6 c5 and these two cancel out, and we are EXACTLY transposing into a main line advance French.
But knowing that we are in a better version than the advance french because of our free tempo (used for Bd3), should inform us that there is a better move than Be2 to defend the d4 pawn, and we should try to work a bit to find the correct move.
At decent depth, the engine like dxc5 b4.
Conceptually, we instinctively dislike dxc5 in general in the French because we played c3 to take with cxd4 in case of cxd4 to maintain our pawn structure intact. Another reason is the theme we find in QGDs of making black waste half a tempo with a move like Be7 fist before taking on c5.
Another way to think about this position is that it can also come from a mainline Bd3 french where black played Be7 in his last move and there dxc5 becomes a clear candidate move.
There is also another idea that we find in many main line advance french lines, which is to gambit a pawn by allowing black to go cxd4 and dxc3 in a row and to use that time for moves like Nbd2 or a3 b4. But here the engines seems quite sure that the correct/only move is the counterintuitive dxc5. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. b4 *


