[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/MansourMohamed"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.29"]
[UTCTime "12:36:20"]

1... e6 2. d4 a6 { Nakamura employs this system as black and crushes GMs with either e6 first or sometimes even a6 first.
Most people simply transpose to some sort of french with the odd a6 inclusion, but still aren't able to punish Hikaru.
For instance, one PV of SF16 on Lichess here is to transpose into a classical Nc3 french main line, do the Nce2 c3 maneuver but go into the Nf3 line instead of the f4 (the line that I like over Nf3). And we end up in a very main position with a slight twist of a6 from black (as if it was played in the last black move). This is shocking because it means even with e6 on move 1, this is the best white can get out.
This study will serve two purposes: one to know how to counter this line itself and two to really master the plans in French if we get a tempo up (a6 is useful but at the first order it is like a quasi passing move). This theme (of a tempo up french) is very useful because it can happen in many different ways, e6 d6 d5, e6 c6 d5 c5 or here with a6 d5 which does not necessarily loose a tempo per say but leaves white with maximum flexibility.
Here white has Be3, Nc3,Bd3 and Nf3 (in that order according to the CCD but all 4 moves are equivalent in terms of strength at around +0.2 for white).
Bd3 is by far the simplest move out of the 4 options because in all likelihood it will transpose back to a main line advance french position but with a6 included for black and Bd3 included for white (which is one of the main moves in the resulting positions for black and Bd3 is the top move for white vs a6), in other words very familiar territory. } 3. Bd3 { This is one of the top CCD line and quite understandable for humans, since Bd3 is the ideal square in french-like setups (or also in e6 b6 Bb7 setups for that matter).
The main line for black here leads to a main line advance french position but with the inclusion of a6 and Bd3, a main move in the resulting advance french main line for black and the top response for white for white (with Bd3), so white should still know it. } (3. Be3 { The preferred move of the engine after e6 but can lead to many different directions including some positions that look like an advance french or some positions that look like Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4. This is the most ambitious try for white but also requires some precise analysis and effort from the white side to understand the nuances. } 3... d5 { Here already Nd2, Nc3 and e5 are all very strong for white and is very hard to decide between them.
for the CCD, Nd2 and Nc3 are equivalent and the top moves (that can transpose after dxe4 but not necessarily) while e5 is still good but considerably weaker. } 4. Nc3 (4. Nd2 dxe4 5. Nxe4 { This looks like some version of the Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 French line. }) (4. e5 c5 5. c3 { This looks like an advance French side line where white went Be3 on a6 in the main line advance french tabia (after c5 c3). }) 4... dxe4 5. Nxe4 { This looks like some version of the Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 French line. }) (3. Nf3 { This is yet another possibility out of the 4 moves mentioned before.
Nf3 eliminates any f4 Nc3 Nce2 c3 lines but the hope for white here is to transpose back to a main line advance french line with something like d5 e5 c5 c3 Nc6.
We can also still get the Nc3 Nce2 c3 setup(without the f4) if black goes Nf6.
For those reasons, overall Nf3 here is very strong. } 3... d5 { White has two options here and both are very good :
either go for Nc3 hoping for Nf6 e5 Nfd7 Ne2 c5 c3 to get a very pleasant position (and a very similar one to our absolute main line in the french with Nc3 Nce2 c3, the only difference is that we go f4 there but here we already played Nf3, this is not a problem at all objectively because both Nf3 and f4 were the the two main moves in the main line after Nc3 Nce2 c3 but this means we need to familiarize ourselves with the Nf3 line a bit since its plan are a bit different form the f4 position plan).
Or go for e5 right away and in all likelihood, get a main line advance French position where we should be really comfortable and happy. } 4. Nc3 (4. e5 c5 5. c3 Nc6 { Here already we just transposed into a mainline advance french position if you consider as if black just played a6 from the main line advance french tabia.
Here the correct move is Bd3, a move we should already know from our advance french analysis. } 6. Bd3) 4... Nf6 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Ne2 c5 7. c3 { This is somewhat of a dream for white since we got to play the classic Nc3 Nce2 c3 maneuver in good conditions. The fact that we didn't get f4 before Nf3 is not really an issue since if we remember correctly in the Nc3 Nce2 c3 main line both f4 and Nf3 were main moves for white.
White should be very happy here. }) 3... d5 4. e5 (4. Nc3 { Another possibility shown by the engine but I don't really recommend it. It leads to a very strange and forcing tactical sequence that you need to memorize with a sudden Bxf2+ Kxf2 in the mix and we are very far from a french like position. } 4... c5 5. dxc5 Bxc5 6. exd5 exd5 7. Nxd5 Bxf2+ 8. Kxf2 Qxd5 9. Qf3 { This is the best line shown by the engine after c5 starting with Nc3. This is a very forcing and tactical solution which is very far from any french positional themes and for this reason I reject it in favor of the more thematic and positional move e5 instead of Nc3. }) 4... c5 5. c3 Nc6 6. Nf3 { We reached a main line of the advance french with the inclusion of a6 Bd3, an inclusion that favors white.
We should have analyzed the advance French very deeply and thus we should be very happy to get this position from a different move order.
Keep in mind that we don't really lay the advance french vs the french from black(our main weapon is Nc3 Nce2 c3 instead) but we prepare it in case we get some transpositions form the Sicilian (ideally with c5 into e6 move order, but sometimes even with a Nf3 Nc6 c3 move order. As of this date, I stopped trying to force a french out of the Scandinavian because I became convinced that it is too risky and not a fundamentally sound approach for white). } *


