1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 { Up until now this is our main line vs the Pirc/Modern. We employ the same setup of Nc3 e3 Qd2 and often times O-O-O, and we hope to maximize the potential transpositions out of any other move orders (like here out the Pirc). } 3... e6 { Note that we can get this d6 e6 / e6 d6 right away (i.e. in two moves) without including any other moves. And since we are in a French chapter, black will go d6 d5 at some point transposing into a French like structure with a tempo down. } 4. Be3 d5 { Black obviously lost a tempo with d7 d6 into d6 d5 and is transposing to a French like structure and potentially some version of a main french position plus or minus a tempo.
Here engine likes Bd3 allowing mass exchanges on e4 and the simple yet efficient plan of Nd7 Nf6 winning a tempo on our LSB on e4. But as a French player e5 is a no-brainer because we are getting a tempo up Nc3 c3 Nce2 French main line with the free inclusion of Be3, a typical good move in those positions. } 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Nce2 c5 7. c3 Nc6 8. f4 { Again we go for the f4 over Nf3 lines because we favor those vs the main lines, so a fortiori they work here even better since we are up a free tempo used for Be3.
This is a tempo up mainline Nc3 Nce2 c3 french, a line we are extremely happy to play as white (imagine you get this out of 1.e4 every time).
We can continue/push the analysis a bit, include normal Nc3 Nce2 c3 french me from here for both side like Be7 Nf3 Qb6 Qd2 for example. } 8... Be7 9. Nf3 Qb6 10. Qd2 O-O { In this position, if it were up to black to play, we know that f6 g6 is decent (0.00) but that f6 h4 is the line of engine (still 0.00).
At lower depth, engine like h4 for white. Even h4 f6 is the top line which a clear tempo of a very important Nc3 Nce2 c3 French main line, so white can't be too unhappy (it's +0.6 now at lower depth as opposed to the regular version, at tempo down which is a 0.00). } 11. h4 *


[Event "White Vs [French]: Opening [Nc3 Bb4][Advance Winawer][7.h4][Wide]"]
