[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Gg4E2sIS/7JcMRl16"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/MansourMohamed"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3p4/3PP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2025.04.04"]
[UTCTime "18:01:18"]

1... Bb4 { The black threat is simply to win the e4 pawn with dxe4 since its defender, the c3 knight, is now pinned. And there is really no good way to defend the pawn.
exd5 is possible to get rid of that pawn, but e5, saving it and gaining more space in the spirit of the French, is by a huge margin the best move (according to the CCD).
Just keep in mind that after e5 we can't really apply our intuition and knowledge acquired from the advance or the Nc3 Nce2 french, as the positions that follow e5 are really tactical with a very high price for a move.
To navigate these lines, white needs a good deal of memory, since there are so few easy/natural moves.
I personally have great trouble navigating these lines even even if I get good positions out of the opening (and for this reason I sometimes choose exd5 Bd3 here to get a game and quickly get out of theory).
Nevertheless, its is hugely rewarding to study these lines as if black goes O-O at some point they simply become a very pleasant risk free mating attack on the black king.
h4 h5 h6 is in general a very common theme in most of the upcoming lines.
By far the most common/fundamental tabia that we should expect/deeply analyze is after e5 c5 a3 Bxc3+ bxc3, and there black have many options like Ne7 and Qc7. } 2. e5 { This is (e5) the best try according to engines and the CCD.
After a forced sequence of moves (c5 a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 and then either Qc7 or Ne7), instead of learning the very tactical engine lines that start with Qg4 Qxg7 Qxh7 which are a mess to understand and have been analyzed to death, the CCD suggests another very good positional move as its top choice : h4,
h4 is a universally good move in a lot of these Nc3 Bb4 French lines. For instance, according to the CCD, h4 is the top move vs Ne7, Qc7 and b6.
Black has 3 main options here : c5, Ne7 and b6.
Ne7 and c5 right away can (and probably will) transpose into each other. For example, Ne7 here, we go a3 and after Bxc3+ bxc3, black will in all likelihood play c5, and we transpose back into the main line move order with c5 right away. Ne7 and c5 are equivalent, b6 is slightly inferior to both.
Another move to maybe think about is Bxc3+, but even without any analysis, it is clear that we are getting a better version then the main line (compare it to c5 a3 Bxc3+ bxc3). } 2... c5 { The absolute main line, black keeps his options open but going c5 first and deciding what line to chose from a bit later (say between Ne7 lines or b6 lines or Qd7 lines etc.). } (2... Ne7 { Nothing to worry about just yet because Ne7 is a superficial move order because we will probably transpose after a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 c5.
If black desperately wants an independent position from the main line after a3 Bxc3+ bxc3, he can always play something different like b6, h6 or maybe even O-O. In that case, we can always go back to fundamentally sound/generic anti Winawer white moves like Qg4 Bd3 and h4. } 3. a3 Bxc3+ 4. bxc3 { Here c5 is the main move transposing back into the mainline, but b6 is also possible to aim for a different position as black. } 4... c5 { We transposed back into the mainline with another move order, as usual, we choose h4 here. The idea is simple, if possible, we want h4 h5 h6 g6 to create dark square wholes around the black king and exploit them with our DSB (with a move like Bg5) since black has lost his DSB. Also after h4, Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 ideas are bit stronger now because the h pawn becomes a passer in those lines and is after h4 already closer to promotion. } (4... b6 { I had this position vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at Lv92(Lv100 = 2300 ELO), The game continued 7. h4 O-O 8. Bd3 Ba6 9. Nf3 c5 10. Bxh7+ Kh8 11. Ng5 Ng8 12. Bxg8 Kxg8 13. Qh5 Bd3 14. cxd3 Qxg5 15. hxg5 f5 16. g6 cxd4 17. Qh7#
Engine likes Qg4 best here but h4 is also very strong.
The reason for Qg4 working here is quite simple, black does not have his usual Qc7 Qc3+ because he did not play c5, or in other words Qg4 simply wins a pawn or two(on g7 /h7) for free. But apparently not after 5. Qg4 Nf5 Bd3 h5 and our queen retreats but the engine gives +1.5 for white at lower depth.
Apparently the only slight problem with h4 is that it gives black a chance to equalize with Ba6. But also the more I look at different lines, even the Qg4 engine line, black is getting Ba6 at some point in all these lines so the question is to let Ba6 happen in good conditions for white.
In other words we don't have the luxury of passing(with h4) since Bh6 is coming fast. In the main line(c5 instead of b6), both h4 and Qg4 are main moves and in the Qg4 line, white goes Qg4 B3 h5 Qf5 and the idea is that whenever black goes for b6 Ba6 we take twice on f5 and win a pawn. Compare that line with this one, black really wants something like Ba6 on Qg4 to exchange LSBs but he is not in time because of Qxg7. For example, 5. Qg4 Ba6 6. Qxg7 Rg8 7. Qxh7 Bxf1 8. Kxf1 c5 is +3.5 for white. } 5. Qg4 Ba6 6. Qxg7 Rg8 7. Qxh7 Bxf1 8. Kxf1 c5 { h4 is +3.7 } 9. h4) (4... O-O { The third most common move online for black after c5 and b6.
O-O is on its face risky because the white attack looks dangers and immediate with moves like Bd3 Bxh7+ h4 Nf3 Ng5 Qh5 etc. A concrete question for white is that is he in time for Bd3 c5 say h4 or Nf3 and c5 Bxh7+(or is Be2 good enough). After palying with the engine Bd3 c5 Nf3 c4 Bxh7+ works here for white but it is not self evident and requires a bit of calculation. } 5. Bd3 Ng6 6. h4 { This theme is very common in many french lines h4 woks even if it under-protected because of a combination of opening the h file Bxh7+ and Qh5. } 6... Nxh4 { Bxh7+ is the most forcing option and wins on the spot, Qh5 is a bit worse but also wins. } 7. Bxh7+ (7. Qh5 g6 (7... Nxg2+ 8. Kf1 Re8 9. Qxh7+ Kf8 10. Kxg2) 8. Qh6 Nxg2+ 9. Kf1 Re8 10. Bxg6 hxg6 (10... fxg6 11. Qxh7+ Kf8 12. Bh6#) 11. Qh8#) 7... Kxh7 8. Qh5+ Kg8 9. Rxh4 f5 10. Nf3 Qe8 11. Qh7+ Kf7 12. Ng5+ Ke7 13. Qxg7+ Kd8 14. Rh7 Nd7 15. Ne4 fxe4 16. Bg5+ Rf6 (16... Qe7 17. Qxe7#) 17. Bxf6+ Nxf6 18. Qxc7#) 5. h4) (2... b6 { The third best move after c5 and Ne7, a bit of a sideline but a serious one.
The idea is quite deep: not to go Bb7 at some point and stare at the d5 black pawn, but to prepare c5 in even better conditions(c5 right away was already possible) but more importantly to neutralize the WLSB and maybe even gain a tempo at the same time. The idea is either Ba6 right away or wait for the WLSB to go to its preferred spot on d3 and then play Ba6 to force Bxa6 Nxa6 winning a tempo and getting rid of a monster attacking piece.
The moves to consider here for white are Qg4, h4 and a3. Let's look at a3 first because it may get us a better version of the main line, say a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 c5 and we are in a very known side line (it is if black played b6 last in the main Winawer tabia). But black can deviate with Qd7 after a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 so we need to know what we are doing.
Qg4 is an all in move and in general, needs to be deeply calculated. As a rule of thumb, before Bxc3+ bxc3 white tends to avoid Qg4 because black can simply meet it with Bf8.
This leaves us with h4 but it is not very clear why h4 (or the inclusion of h4 h5 for both sides) is the the top engine move in this particular position. h4 is always a good/thematic move in many french variation including the Nc3 Nce2 c3 lines and of course in many Winawer variations. The basic motif behind h4 is h4 h5 h6 g6 Bg5 for example to exploit the wholes in the dark squares. h4 is begging black to go Bxc3+ now so that his dark squares in the king side are forever weak. This is why the engine prefers h4 first to take away Bf8 from black. An ideal line would be : h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 c5 and now either h4 h5 h6 Bg5 or Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7.
Another reason why h4 prepares Qg4 and not vice versa is that after a sample line like h4 Bxc3+ bxc3 say c5 Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7, now the white h pawn is a passer and is already on h4 so is a bit faster for queening. } 3. h4) (2... Bxc3+ { This is a strictly worse version for black than the main line with c5 first, inducing a3 and then going Bxc3+. We can understand this quite simply : after bxc3 c5, if white goes a3 we transpose EXACTLY back into the main line of the Winawer, but white is not forced to go a3 in that position and can instead go Qg4 instead of a3 with a +1.2 advantage already.
Qg4 is thematic in many Winawer lines but it is never clear a priori whether Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 favors white or black(it is actually highly dependent on the situation). But here we are getting the Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 line with a tempo up compared to the mainlines, so we can suspect that white is doing very well.
We can also view this form another angle, in many lines in the Winawer, white often ends up going a3 a4 to expand queen side and to take the a4 square away from the black queen (say after Qa5) and in doing so he would have lost a tempo compared to this version where we can go a2 a4 in one go in the future if we ever need that resource. } 3. bxc3 { c5 transposes to the main line with a tempo up for white.
c5 a3 transposes EXACTLY back to the main line, but this is the least ambitious choice for white. } 3... c5 { The moves we consider here are Qg4, h4 and Nf3.
Nf3 is instinctively inferior unless there is a concrete tactical reason because we know in many Winawer lines white goes Qg4 and Nf3 blocks Qg4.
Qg4 is going for the kill but a priori, there is no clear way of evaluating the many Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 positions, all we know is that, compared to the main lines, we are a tempo up after Qxh7 so white can't be that bad in the resulting position (white is in fact +2 after Qxh7), but we need to know what we are doing in order to convert after Qg4.
h4 is more positional and invites a long positional battle in a typical french position where both sides are probably already out of theory, if white is not just memorizing moves and knows the general middle game ideas of typical Winawer french, after h4 white should be the huge favorite. A typical continuation looks something like h4, h5 (to stop both white h5 and Qg4) and now simply Nf3 and we have a game. } 4. h4 (4. Qg4 Ne7 5. Qxg7 Rg8 6. Qxh7 { A priori, there is no clear way of evaluating the many Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 positions, all we know is that compared to the main lines, we are a tempo up after Qxh7 so white can't be that bad in the resulting position (white is in fact +2 after Qxh7), but we need to know what we are doing in order to convert after Qg4. })) 3. a3 { There are some "pathological" lines with moves like cxd4 or Ba5 or even Qa5. Those are definitely important to know to cover all bases but our focus should be on the tabia after Bxc3+ bxc3. } 3... Bxc3+ 4. bxc3 { This is arguably the starting position / the first and fundamental tabia of the Nc3 Bb4 French also called the Winawer (since the sequence c5 a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 is pretty much forced for both sides).
The first immediate remake is that black could have forced this same position but without a3 if he wanted to (with Bxc3+, unprovoked, and then c5 a move later). Or in other words, inducing a3 favors black. Or in other words, this move order (of c5 right away) is the most precise for black, which signals that black knows what he is doing to some extent.
Here, black has many options.
-The main line at depth 60+ is Ne7 Qg4.
-Qc7 first is also a move and can transpose with Ne7 first.
-b6 is a non trivial try for black.
-On Qa5, Bd2 is a very thematic way to deal with Qa5 in all these lines, and white is doing fine.
Instead, of learning all the absurdly complicated and convoluted theory on Ne7 starting with Qg4, the CCD suggest a very deep idea that works universally in a lot of Nc3 Bb4 french lines (not just vs Ne7) : h4. h4 works (i.e. is the top reply) vs Ne7 Qc7 and b6, for example.
Starting from this tabia, our goal should be to try to make h4 work no matter what black plays here. } 4... Ne7 { One of the top moves for black along with Qc7.
Again there is the Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 lines but they are a tactical and move by move total mess. Instead, as we try to do in every positions after Bxc3+ bxc3, our instinct should be to try to make h4 work at all costs, and it turns out that h4 is also the top engine move here.
Note that Ne7 and Qc7 are often played one after the other in the Bxc3+ bxc3 tabia in both move orders leading to the same resulting position : Ne7 h4 Qc7 or Qc7 h4 Ne7. } (4... Qc7 { We said that we wanted to systematically try to make h4 work in all positions after Bxc3+ bxc3, so the question here is can we make h4 work. First line to know/to calculate is h4 cxd cxd Qc3+ Bd2 Qxd4.
Again, according to the CCD and like in the Ne7 line, h4 also works here and is the top engine move (and note that we can even immediately transpose between lines with h4 Ne7).
The only slight "problem" with h4 here, is that will transform the french from a slow positional struggle into a fast and tactical gambit after h4 cxd cxd Qc3+ Bd2 Qxd4, and we need to be prepared for that. } 5. h4 { The first line to have/to calculate here is what happens after cxd4. If we see cxd4 cxd4 Qc3+ Bd2 Qxd4 we might stop calculating but this is in fact the refuting white line that we need to know.
h4 here in fact is a gambit, we loose the d4 pawn but white gains a lot of activity as compensations and in some lines we can even trap the black queen. After h4 the fight becomes dynamic and tactical, so we need to know the lines by heart or calculate from scratch really fast. } 5... cxd4 6. cxd4 Qc3+ 7. Bd2 Qxd4 { Forced line to know/have before playing h4. } 8. Nf3 Qe4+ { Engines like Be3 here, it also wants Bd3 next.
To know whether Be3 or Be2 is the move in many similar lines (after cxd cxd Qc3+ Bd2 Qxd4 Nf3 Qe4+) keep in mind that if the black knight is already on e7 then Be3 can't be correct because Nf5 will come next and shortly after Nxe3+ fxe3 and Qxe3+. Here on the other hand the black knight is still on its starting square so we have the luxury of Be3 and Bd3 next because Ne7 Nf5 Nxe3 is way too slow and not in time.
Imagine this extract position with a black knight on e7, in that case the move is Be2 instead of Be3. Otherwise, Be3 Nf5 and Bd3 is not in time because of Nxe3 attacking the white queen. } 9. Be3 Ne7 (9... Nc6 10. Bd3 Qg4 11. h5 { Thematic move in many Qc3+ Qxd4 Qg4 lines, the threat is trap the black queen with the simple yet deadly Rh4. } 11... Qxg2 { Traps the BQ. } 12. Rh2 (12. Rh4 Nge7 13. Bf1 Qxf1+ 14. Kxf1) 12... Qg4 13. Rh4 Qg2 14. Bf1 Qxf1+ 15. Kxf1) 10. Bd3 Qg4 { Here you need to know that g2 is not really en pris because the black queen will get trapped if it captures on g2, but calculating the concrete way of trapping the black queen (after a passing move like say Rb8) is harder then it looks and requires some basic technique. } 11. h5 { The best move in the position gaining space and threatening to win the black queen with Rh4. } (11. Rh3 { g2 is still tactically protected and on Nf5 we can take on f5 and Rg3 Rxg7 at the very least. } 11... Qxg2 { Traps the black queen. } 12. Rg3 Qh1+ 13. Bf1 Nf5 14. Rh3 (14. Rg1 Qxg1 15. Nxg1) 14... Qxh3 15. Bxh3) 11... Nf5 { Stops Rh4 and Rg3 in some lines winning the black queen if it captures on g2. In other words g2 is now really unprotected.
Possible moves here Bxf5 or Kf1. } (11... Qxg2 { Traps the black queen. } 12. Rh2 Qg4 13. Rh4 Qg2 14. Bf1 Qxf1+ 15. Kxf1) (11... Nbc6 { Does not answer the white threat, white can now trap the black queen. } 12. Rh4 Qxg2 13. Bf1 Qxf1+ 14. Kxf1) 12. Kf1 { White is down a pawn but is extremely active, even after Nxe3+ fxe3. } (12. Bxf5 { White gives us his LSB and the BP and is down a pawn but is so ahead in development that Bxf5 is till very strong. })) (4... c4 { In general locking the queen side this early simplifies the white task tremendously which is now is reduced to creating an attack/chaos in the king side without any worries to what might happen in the queen since it is now closed forever, even a5 b5 b4 won't achieve anything since the b4 square is protected by by two white pawns. This is explained to beginners by connecting the pawns with arrows to determine which side is playing on which side. By this method, it becomes visually clear that white wants to play in the king side while black wants to play in the queen side, with the only problem that there is no play in the queen side after c4.
Fundamentally a very bad move here but sometimes possible after a couple of moves.
It is instructive to know how to punish c4 just not for the sake of remembering a concrete variation but to get a taste of the ideas of the position for white especially if black gives us a free tempo or even better plays a committal and mistimed move like c4.
c4 for black or c5 for white freezing the central tension in of itself is mostly bad in most French and even in most QGDs, with a few exceptions here and there (the a3 Qb6 advance french line and the Bf4 Nbd7 instead of c5 QGD to name a few).
The way to punish c4 varies a lot from QGD positions, advance french, Nce2 french and here in the Winawer.
Here there are no concrete ways to solely punish c4,but we treat c4 as passing move, i.e. white execute his usual threat as if black has passed. The main and most immediate white threat is the very thematic in many french lines, where the BDSB left the defense of g7: Qg4.
The generic h4 is also very good. } 5. Qg4 (5. h4)) 5. h4 { This h4 move became my current repertoire the moment I discovered (thanks to the CCD) that h4 is very thematic in many Nc3 Bb4 lines (not just vs Ne7, for example, it also works vs Qc7 and b6) and it saves me the trouble of learning by heart the 20 move tactical variations that come after Qg4.
Having the stamp of approval from engines to h4 here and in many Nc3 Bb4 lines is the extra push I needed to adopt it in my repertoire because conceptually h4 is always a move we want to play in any French structure. This could be understood very simply because if we get h4 h5 h6 g6 in a French structure, black will end up with dark square holes around his king which will be weak for a very long time without a DSB whether he ends castling king side or queen side. This fact is also well understood in "modern defence" setups : g6 d6 is recommended and is strong, whereas e6 g6 is fundamentally weak and often punished with h4 h5. Also this is even better/stronger since black has lost his DSB so we have the monopoly on dark squares for the rest of the game. Also h4 in some sense prepares Qg4 in the future, because now in all the Qg4 Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 lines, the h pawn becomes a passer and is two squares closer to queening. Another reason is the after we get the h4 h5 h6 g6 pawn structure, black will never have the f6 pawn break and with that will never have any pawn break for the rest of the game. In other words, white will have risk free eternal advantage that he can play for hours without any downside. } 5... Qc7 { Note that this position is really important because we could have gotten it by the two move orders Ne7 first or Qc7 first. Also it comes just after the fundamental Winawer tabia of c5 a3 Bxc3+ bxc3.
Here a lot of moves are possible, for example Nf3, a move I would play in a bullet game, and it is objectively a good move, Or Qg4 now but we need to calculate the Qxg7 Rg8 Qxh7 lines very w, but the deepest move is the mysterious Rh3 according to the CCD.
Stockfish at higher depth has reached the same conclusion:
SF 15.1 D48 Rh3 Nbc6 h5 +0.51
SF 16 D52 Rh3 Nbc6 h5 +0.28
Rh3 here is inexplicable and impossible to find or understand for a human, so if we want to play it, we need immense prep/memory of these lines.
Another obvious question to ask is why not h4 h5 h6 right away? For me continuing with h5, h6 makes a lot more sense if black went O-O the previous move instead of Qc7 because there the smoke clears and our concepts of dark square's weakness around the black king coupled with white having a DSB and black lacking a DSB, come to life right away. Here on the other hand, black is temporizing/buying time, so we should do the same with Rh3, at least for one move.
First lines two lines to calculate are h5 and Qg4.
On h4 the first thing to have is what happens if black goes for the Qc3+ and Qxd4 line, or in other words does the gambit work here (we know for example that it works in the same position without the inclusion of the Ne7 : 7. h4 cxd4 8. cxd4 Qc3+ 9. Bd2 Qxd4 10. Nf3 Qe4+ 11. Be3 Ne7 12. Bd3 Qg4). It turns out that h5 is also decent here and leads to a very similar position to the one mentioned before, after : 8. h5 cxd4 9. cxd4 Qc3+ 10. Bd2 Qxd4 11. Nf3 Qe4+ 12. Be2 Nf5 13. Kf1, the key difference is that in that line we need Be2 instead of Be3 because of the inclusion of Ne7 facilitating Nf5 into Nxe3.
Qg4 on the other hand looses at the very least a pawn without any compensation after cxd4 or even even a whole rook after cxd cxd Qc3+ Bd2.
Nf3 is always a question mark in these positions because we never know where Nf3 is good vs bad because it blocks Qg4.
What's left is Bd2 and Rb1 anticipating cxd4. While they are both decent they are a bit artificial since we are not really sure that a8 rook belongs on b1 and we know that Bd2 is often an anti-Qa5 move but besides that we might go Bg5 in one go after h4 h5 h6 g6.
So it turns out that in some sense Rh3 is the only move here that makes sense, stopping all the cxd cxd Qc3+ ideas.
Engine like Rh3 a bit better but h5 going for the Qc3+ line is also good. Vs the crazyBishop android app at lv100, I always go h5 and it greeds for the pawn and we end up in messy positions. If you go for h5, you need to know some deep details like when to Be2 vs Be3, the well timed Kf1, etc. } (5... c4 { Again, we are checking c4 for black at every move.
We already saw one version where c4 makes sense for black : in case black already castled in the queen side (7. h4 Qc7 8. Rh3 Nbc6 9. h5 h6 10. Nf3 Bd7 11. Be2 O-O-O 12. Kf1 c4 ).
Contrary to the c4 line without the inclusion of Ne7, i.e. c4 right away(and where we refute c4 by starting with Qg4), after the inclusion of Ne7 h4, Qg4 right away is no longer the best move (It's still winning) but the CCD favor h5 instead for some reason.
The mechanism of action of h pawn push is quite similar to main lines, where we go h4 h5 h6 g6 and finally Bg5 to exploit the newly created dark square weakness around the black king side. After h4 h5 h6 g6, castling king side for black is tantamount to suicide. A sample line would be 7. h4 c4 8. h5 Nbc6 9. h6 g6 10. Bg5 Qa5 11. Qd2 +2.5. } 6. h5 Nbc6 7. h6 g6 8. Bg5 Qa5 9. Qd2 { This is already +2.5. }) (5... Nbc6 { Nc6 here is more of a sideline but a very sound looking move. The engine here wants desperately to get the h5 h6 g6 setup as fast as possible. A sample line would be 8. h5 Bd7 9. h6 g6 10. Bg5 O-O 11. Qd2 .
I won this position vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at MAX Lv (Lv100) : 7. h4 Nbc6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 c4 10. Bxh7+ Kh8 11. Ng5 g6 12. h5 Kg7 13. hxg6 ...
Nf3 and h5 are the two top moves in this position with the engine slightly preferring h5 first. The nuances are almost impossible to determine for a human but here is a flavor :
6. h5 Qa5 7. Bd2 Qc7 8. Nf3 h6
6. Nf3 Qa5 7. Bd2 h6 8. h5 Qc7
6. Nf3 h6 7. h5 Qa5 8. Bd2 Qc7
All these move orders are optimal for both sides and lead to the same end position but they are slightly different from the stand point of the engine.
For a human, if we can't find a tactical refutation of the greedy/positional h5 we should go for it. Typically something with cxd cxd Qc7.
Another move to look at first is Qg4 since it would be played in good conditions here (since the h pawn is closer to queening after h4 and there are no Qc7 Qc3+ Qxa1 exploiting the fact that our a1 rook is unprotected. But the engine refutes Qg4 with a very simple and thematic idea : Qg4 Nf5 Bd3 h5 Qd1 and white has achieved nothing. } 6. h5 (6. Nf3 { Nf3 was the move I chose in my game vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at MAX Lv (Lv100) : 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 c4 10. Bxh7+ Kh8 11. Ng5 g6 12. h5 Kg7 13. hxg6 ... } 6... Qa5 (6... h6 7. h5 Qa5 8. Bd2 Qc7) (6... O-O { A crazy move to see as a human because we immediately see greek gift themes with Bd3 Ng5 Qh5 etc.
This is still my game vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at MAX Lv (Lv100) : 7. h4 Nbc6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 c4 10. Bxh7+ Kh8 11. Ng5 g6 12. h5 Kg7 13. hxg6 ...
Strangely enough this position is not lost just yet for black even though it seems that way for a human. White has 2 main attacking ideas here : either go for h5 h6 g6 Bg5 Qa5 Qd2 cxd cxd Qxd2+ Kxd2 and for some reason this endgame is winning for white (+2), or try to go for a greek gift style attack with Bd3 Ng5 Qa5 etc. but this type of attack does not work with perfect play from black but more importantly, unless black play h6 hxg5 there is no really way to force the attack to work. If black is not careful, maybe some Ng5 Nh7 Nf6 sacrificing the knight might work in some lines : 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Bd3 h6 8. Ng5 Bd7 9. Nh7 Re8 10. Nf6+ gxf6 11. Qg4+ . } 7. Be2 (7. h5) (7. Bd3 { Not the only move and probably not the best but still decent, the goal is to get a quick with a greek git style attack.
This is still my game On [Date "2025.04.06"] vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at MAX Lv (Lv100) : 7. h4 Nbc6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 c4 10. Bxh7+ Kh8 11. Ng5 g6 12. h5 Kg7 13. hxg6 ... } 7... c4 { Begging for a greek gift and it turns out that it works here.
This is still my game On [Date "2025.04.06"] vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at MAX Lv (Lv100) : 7. h4 Nbc6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 c4 10. Bxh7+ Kh8 11. Ng5 g6 12. h5 Kg7 13. hxg6 ... } 8. Bxh7+ Kh8 (8... Kxh7 9. Ng5+ Kg8 10. Qh5 Re8 11. Qxf7+ Kh8 12. Qh5+ Kg8 { +11 here but still no quick mate.
The quickest wins are Rh3 and a4 (we can also still check for a bit). }) 9. Ng5 { This is still my game On [Date "2025.04.06"] vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at MAX Lv (Lv100) : 7. h4 Nbc6 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 c4 10. Bxh7+ Kh8 11. Ng5 g6 12. h5 Kg7 13. hxg6 ... } 9... g6 10. h5 Kg7 11. hxg6 fxg6 { In my game On [Date "2025.04.06"] vs The Chess Lv.100 Free for Android By UNBALANCE Corporation at MAX Lv (Lv100), I went on with Qg7 instead of the move recommended by the engine Bxh6, I won the game at 69 moves. } 12. Bxg6 (12. Qg4) 12... Kxg6 13. Qh5+ Kf5 14. g4#)) 7. Bd2 h6 8. h5 Qc7) 6... Bd7 (6... Qa5 { A common theme in many Winawer lines is Bd2 as antidote to Qa5, I was this theme in many different variations/move orders. } 7. Bd2 Qc7 { Here the engine, for some deep reasons, is screaming for Qg4 before Nf3.
Nf3 is also good. } 8. Nf3 (8. Qg4 Rg8 9. Nf3) 8... h6) (6... cxd4 { Here, instead of the lazy cxd on cxd, white needs to notice that he can play h6 for free because gxh8=Q+ queens with check!, a sample line would be 8. h5 cxd4 9. h6 dxc3 10. hxg7 Rg8 11. Rxh7. } 7. h6 dxc3 (7... g6 8. cxd4 { We got the inclusion of h6 g6 for free which favors white. }) 8. hxg7 Rg8 9. Rxh7) 7. h6 g6 8. Bg5 Qa5 9. Qd2 { A sample line to illustrate the white plans after getting the ideal setup of h4 h5 h6 g6 Bg5. } 9... cxd4 10. cxd4 Qxd2+ 11. Kxd2 { This is +1.5 for some reason. These types of endgames obtained from the Winawer after h4 h5 h6 g6 Bg4 Qa5 Qd2 cxd cxd Qxd2+ Kxd2 are often times evaluated between +1.5 to +2 even thought there is no clear mating attack/pattern with the queens off, it's just the the positional advantage is overwhelming and eternal for white. }) (5... O-O { This is already lost with perfect play according to the CCD (+0.9 for SF D26).
Intuitively, black should never O-O in this position as the threats of h5 h6 g6 Bg5, maybe greek gift ideas with Bd3 Nf3 Ng5+, maybe Qh5 at some point all of this in total safety since the white king seems pretty safe on e8 since the center is closed.
There are two winning moves here : h5 and Nh3.
Humanly Qg4 Nf3 and Bd3 are also moves to consider.
The attacking ideas for white are simple/straightforward, white wants h4 h5 h6 and on g6 to exploit the dark square weaknesses/holes around the black king with Bg5 Qd2 with ideal mating construction of h6 and Qg2#.
I choose h5 as it is the most direct way to play this position because the alternative Nh3 seems very mysterious to me (for example, what is the nuance/difference between Nh6 and Nf3?).
The first question to answer here is why does Bd3 not work, a sample line would be 8. Bd3 c4 9. Bxh7+ Kxh7 10. Qh5+ Kg8 11. Nf3. Apparently this whole fails because of an only move at the end of it : f6. If black does not find f6, black is mated or white is winning (at least +2).
Qg4 might seem tempting, the idea is to combine it with h5 (stopping Ng6) and Bh6 to win the black rook, a sample line 8. Qg4 Nbc6 9. h5 Bd7 10. Bh6 g6 11. Bxf8 . But Qc7 on Qg4 seems to counter everything with the simple idea of cxd cxd Qc3+ winning the white rook now that the queen no longer defend it.
This is a general theme in many Winawer lines and is even more apparent in the main lines were black already plays Qc7( with or without Ne7), in all those lines white is really discouraged form playing Qg4 not to loose his a1 rook to cxd cxd Qc3+.
By process of elimination h5 is the only move here. } 6. h5 Nbc6 { Not the best move, but a very logical one for black and will help us understand the white king side attack pattern. } 7. h6 { This plan of h4 h5 h6 is very thematic as an antidote the French in general and to the Bb4 line in particular. The point behind h6 is that it forced g6 and by common chess knowledge (for example, imagine a modern position with g6 e6 instead of the solid and normal g6 d6), the g6 e6 combination is structurally/positionally terrible especially if the black king is castled king side. This is aggravated by the fact that black lost his DSB while white still has his.
The first realization is that Bg5 is really strong to exploit these weaknesses after g6. The second one is that if white magically manages to play Qf6, he will win by mating black on g7 with Qg7# supported by the h6 pawn.
On the other hand, if black does not go g6, Qg4 with Qg7# is mate. And on Ng6, we can always take on g7 and go Qh5 to mate black along the semi-open h file. } 7... g6 8. Bg5 { Again a very thematic and direct approach, justifying h4 h5 h6 and controlling the newly created dark square weakness round the black king. And these squares are extra difficult to defend because we have a DSB, while black does not have one.
However, the computer produces a devilish defense that almost refutes Bg5 (but still +2 for white) : Bg5 Qa5 Qd2 cxd4 cxd4 Qxd2+ Kxd2 and black manages to exchange the queens which in turn drastically reduces the chances of black getting mated in the short term. But even after all of this, it is clear that the resulting position after Kd2 strongly favors white (+2) but white goal will be to slowly win a long and superior endgame instead of trying to eat black in 10 or so moves. } 8... Qa5 9. Qd2 cxd4 10. cxd4 Qxd2+ 11. Kxd2 { For some reason this endgame is winning for white. }) 6. Rh3 (6. h5 { a move to know, leads the a gambit that favors white } 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Qc3+ 8. Bd2 Qxd4 9. Nf3 Qe4+ 10. Be2 Nf5 11. Kf1 { Kf1 was the must know detail when we played h5. } 11... Nc6 12. Bd3 Qa4 13. Kg1 (13. Rb1 Qxa3 14. Bc1)) 6... Nbc6 { Very logical developing move from black, now that cxd cxd Qc3+ Qxd4 no longer works.
Here the move suggested by the engine is h5, but it is a bit mysterious why we included Rh3 before h5 since we could have played h5 the previous move(as we saw Rh3 was pretty much the only move in the previous turn by process of elimination).
-Besides h5, Qg4 might be tempting, say a line like Qg4 O-O Bd3 and we may envision a mating attack on black. The problem seems to be cxd cxd and black can sack a knight on e5 for two pawns plus Qxa1 at the end of the line winning the white rook.
-Bd3 is not really an option because it always met with Bd7 and d4 becomes en pris.
-Again Nf3 or Ne2 is always a bit discouraged since it stops Qg4 ideas.
-Rg3 walks into Nf5, protecting g7 and winning a tempo on the rook.
Again, it turns out by process of elimination h5 is the only move for white that makes any sense. } 7. h5 { As usual, we could have reached this position from many different move orders (On the top of my head, with Qc7 first or with Ne7 first, plus the Nc6 move orders). } 7... h6 { Here engines like Nf3 best, but for a human, Qg4, Bd3, Rg3 and maybe even f4 are also to be considered.
The big threat that exists in this position is that as long as we delay Nf3, sacrificing the knight and taking twice on e4 followed by Qxe4+ and Qxa1 wins the white rook.
The first line we try to make work is Qg4 with something like 8. Qg4 Nf5 9. Qf4 Bd7 10. g4, and it looks decent. The engine refutes Qg4 (refutes is a big word, the eval is still 0.00) with Rg8. And white is not in time for Bd3 Bh7 with a line like 8. Qg4 Rg8 9. Bd3 Bd7 10. Bh7 because of cxd4 ( 8. Qg4 Rg8 9. Bd3 cxd4) white has problem defending both d4, c3 and the knight sack on e5 followed by Qxa1.
Bd3 is decent (+0.1) but hangs the d4 pawn after cxd cxd.
Rg3 just looses a tempo for nothing : Rg3 Nf5 Rh3.
f4 is actually decent here, but as we saw in many advanced french lines(as opposed to Nc3 Nce2 c3 french lines) it is usually very hard to tell where f4 is good or not long term. For now f4 Nf5 is met with g4 kicking the knight but this is just a short term consideration. } 8. Nf3 Bd7 9. Be2 { We saw that in many advanced french lines Be2 is the anti Bd7 move and Bd7 is the anti Bd3 move, so Be2 is the move here. Note that thematically, we are often times almost never afraid of Bd3 c4 Be2. } 9... O-O-O 10. Kf1 c4 11. a4 { This was the main line of the engines for both sides after Bb4.
A bit less shocking than going c4 just after a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 because now the black king is sheltered on the queen side so it makes sense to lock the queen side now.
Black is saying that white can't really attack on the queen side with just one pawn, on the other hand white can't really attack on the king side because the white king is there.
This is meant more as a sample line then anything else. } *


